  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Late rallies keep them alive, but winning one-run games three times in five nights is not a sustainable way forward.",
    "what_this_means": "Goldschmidt supplied the power. Two home runs that kept them within reach when Toronto built a two-run lead. That production mattered. It kept the game close enough for Jazz Chisholm to break a tie in the seventh with his own home run. The real problem sits before and after those moments. They left runners on in twelve at-bats and scored on just one of them. The bullpen closed it out clean. Blackburn threw 1.2 scoreless, Cruz struck out three in a shutout frame, Bednar got the save. None of that adds up to a formula. It adds up to narrow escapes. They're 3-7 over the last ten games. One-run wins feel like saves, not signs.",
    "what_to_watch": "Dylan Cease takes the ball tonight for Toronto with a 2.41 ERA. He's one of the division's best and won't leave margin for error the way the Jays did in the late innings.",
